{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0007","statement":"Aristotle classified verbs that denote something happening into three classes: actuality, movement, and action.","source_span":"classified verbs that denote something happening into three classes: actuality, movement and action"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0007","fact_refs":[1],"question":"When and where was the Battle of Fredericksburg fought?","answer":"It was fought December 11–15, 1862, in and around Fredericksburg, Virginia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0007","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"Who commanded the Union Army of the Potomac at the Battle of Fredericksburg, and which Confederate force did they face?","answer":"Maj. Gen. Ambrose E. Burnside commanded the Union Army of the Potomac, facing General Robert E. Lee's Confederate Army of Northern Virginia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0007","fact_refs":[4],"question":"On what date did the Union army launch its frontal assaults at the Battle of Fredericksburg?","answer":"December 13."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0007","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How did Union casualties compare to Confederate casualties at the Battle of Fredericksburg?","answer":"Union casualties were more than twice as heavy as those suffered by the Confederates."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0007","fact_refs":[6],"question":"When was the Wikipedia article on the Battle of Fredericksburg accessed for this paper's example?","answer":"It was accessed on May 5, 2012."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0007","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Into what three classes did Aristotle classify verbs denoting something happening?","answer":"Actuality, movement, and action."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0007","fact_refs":[0],"question":"In which application domains does the paper discuss the use of event detection and extraction systems?","answer":"Summarization, the medical (biomedical) domain, and the context of Twitter posts."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","statement":"The paper proposes generating paraphrases of an input question so that at least one is correctly mapped to a knowledge-base query, bridging the lexicosyntactic gap between questions and knowledge base entries.","source_span":"generating paraphrases of the input question with the goal that at least one of them will be correctly mapped to a knowledge-base query"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","statement":"The proposed grammar model uses latent-variable probabilistic context-free grammars (L-PCFGs) to sample paraphrases and does not require any sentence-aligned paraphrase corpus.","source_span":"leverage the flexibility and scalability of latent-variable probabilistic context-free grammars to sample paraphrases"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","statement":"The evaluation experiments were conducted on the WebQuestions benchmark dataset.","source_span":"Our evaluation experiments on the WebQuestions benchmark dataset show that the performance of the semantic parser significantly improves over strong baselines"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","statement":"The authors estimate the L-PCFG using the spectral method of narayan-15, instead of the EM method used previously by matsuzaki-2005 and petrov-2006.","source_span":"we use the spectral method of narayan-15, instead of using EM, as has been used in the past by matsuzaki-2005 and petrov-2006"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","statement":"The Paralex corpus used to train the L-PCFG contains 18 million pairs of question paraphrases with 2.4 million distinct questions.","source_span":"Paralex is a large monolingual parallel corpus, containing 18 million pairs of question paraphrases with 2.4M distinct questions in the corpus"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","statement":"The treebank used for grammar estimation was constructed by parsing 2.4 million distinct questions from Paralex using the BLLIP parser.","source_span":"We construct a treebank by parsing 2.4M distinct questions from Paralex using the BLLIP parser"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","statement":"The authors experimented with three ways of constructing word lattices: naïve word lattices from the input question, lattices built with the Paraphrase Database (PPDB), and lattices constructed with a bi-layered L-PCFG.","source_span":"We experiment with three ways of constructing word lattices: naïve word lattices representing the words from the input question only, word lattices constructed with the Paraphrase Database BIBREF14 and word lattices constructed with a bi-layered L-PCFG"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Why does the paper generate paraphrases of an input question for semantic parsing?","answer":"So that at least one of the generated paraphrases can be correctly mapped to a knowledge-base query, bridging the gap between how people phrase questions and how the knowledge base encodes facts."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What kind of grammar model does the paper use to generate paraphrases without a sentence-aligned paraphrase corpus?","answer":"A latent-variable probabilistic context-free grammar (L-PCFG) that samples paraphrases, requiring no sentence-aligned paraphrase corpus."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which benchmark dataset was used to evaluate the semantic parser in this paper?","answer":"The WebQuestions benchmark dataset."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What estimation method did the authors use for their L-PCFG, and which prior works used EM instead?","answer":"They used the spectral method of narayan-15; EM had previously been used by matsuzaki-2005 and petrov-2006."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How large is the Paralex corpus used to train the L-PCFG in this paper?","answer":"It contains 18 million pairs of question paraphrases with 2.4 million distinct questions."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How was the treebank for learning the L-PCFG constructed?","answer":"By parsing the 2.4 million distinct questions from the Paralex corpus using the BLLIP parser."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0008","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What three types of word lattices did the authors experiment with for constraining paraphrase generation?","answer":"Naïve word lattices using only the input question's words, lattices built with the Paraphrase Database (PPDB), and lattices constructed with a bi-layered L-PCFG."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","statement":"Urban legends are stories about rare and exceptional events, told as true and just plausible enough to be believed, that tend to propagate virally across communities.","source_span":"Urban legends are a genre of modern folklore, consisting of stories about rare and exceptional events, just plausible enough to be believed, which tend to propagate inexorably across communities"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","statement":"The acronym \"SUCCES\", describing characteristics that make an idea sticky, comes from the concept of \"stickiness\" popularized by the book \"The Tipping Point\".","source_span":"the authors discuss the idea of \"stickiness\" popularized by the book \"The Tipping Point\" BIBREF3... by following the acronym \"SUCCES\""}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","statement":"The \"sewer alligator\" urban legend originally appeared in New York City before appearing in different cities.","source_span":"the sewer alligator, that originally \"appeared\" in New York City BIBREF1 , also appeared in different cities to suit regional variations"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","statement":"The television series \"1000 Ways to Die\" recreates unusual supposed deaths and debunked urban legends in a way similar to the Darwin Awards.","source_span":"the television series \"1000 Ways to Die\"... that recreates unusual supposed deaths and debunked urban legends in a way similar to the Darwin Awards"}
