{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","statement":"Four main patterns are recognized for detecting online propagandists or fake accounts, including sending high volumes of tweets over short periods.","source_span":"Four main patterns are recognized: (i) sending high volumes of tweets over short periods"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How does the paper define an urban legend?","answer":"As a genre of modern folklore consisting of stories about rare and exceptional events, told as true and just plausible enough to be believed, which tend to spread virally across communities."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What acronym describes the characteristics that make an idea \"sticky\" according to the book referenced in the paper, and which book popularized the stickiness concept?",
"answer":"The acronym \"SUCCES\", from the concept of stickiness popularized by the book \"The Tipping Point\"."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Where did the \"sewer alligator\" urban legend originally appear?","answer":"It originally appeared in New York City, before also appearing in different cities with regional variations."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What does the TV series \"1000 Ways to Die\" do, and what is it compared to in the paper?","answer":"It recreates unusual supposed deaths and debunked urban legends, in a way similar to the Darwin Awards."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Who reported the \"Kidney Theft\" urban legend example cited in the paper, and whose signature appeared on it?","answer":"It was reported by snopes.com and signed by a presumed Jerry Mayfield."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0009","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is one of the four patterns used to recognize propagandists or fake accounts that push viral content?","answer":"Sending high volumes of tweets over short periods."}
